age 2 (inline, following the text above)) [HIGH CONFIDENCE: 0.96 — Fully trust and extract visible UI buttons, IDs, and parameters]:\nSCREEN / CONTEXT: CoreID Admin Console > Users  \nUI ACTION: Select user 'J. Employee' (SOL-04821) to view details  \nVISIBLE ENTITIES: Search: SOL-04821 | User: J. Employee | ID: SOL-04821 | Department: Finance | Status: Locked  \nERRORS: None visible  \nCONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.96  \nCONFIDENCE LEVEL: High  \nCONFIDENCE REASON: Clear legible text and distinct UI elements\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [LOW CONFIDENCE: 0.00 / UNRELIABLE — Rely strictly on surrounding written SOP text and do not guess unverified parameters]:\nVLM Analysis FAILED (non-retryable error): [503] {'message': 'ResourceExhausted: Worker local total request limit reached (16/16)', 'type': 'Service Unavailable', 'code': 503}\n{'error': {'message': 'ResourceExhausted: Worker local total request limit reached (16/16)', 'type': 'Service Unavailable', 'code': 503}, 'status': 503}\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [HIGH CONFIDENCE: 0.98 — Fully trust and extract visible UI buttons, IDs, and parameters]:\nSCREEN / CONTEXT: CoreID Admin Console > Users > Reset Password — J. Employee  \nUI ACTION: Click “Generate Temporary Password” with “Force change at next sign-in” checkbox checked  \nVISIBLE ENTITIES: Temporary password = Tq7#mVr2!kLp9x; Checkbox “Force change at next sign-in” checked; Signed in as A. Rao (L1 Support — Password Reset Admin)  \nERRORS: None visible  \nCONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.98  \nCONFIDENCE LEVEL: High  \nCONFIDENCE REASON: All text is clear, legible, and the required elements are distinctly visible.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [HIGH CONFIDENCE: 0.96 — Fully trust and extract visible UI buttons, IDs, and parameters]:\nWe need to extract core procedural action and visible entities. The screenshot shows a ticket resolution page. The procedural action likely \"Mark ticket as Resolved\" or \"Review resolution notes\". But we need to extract exact entity names, IDs, usernames, ticket numbers, checkboxes, status badges, input values.\n\nVisible entities:\n- Application: CoreID Admin Console\n- Page: Ticket #SOL-84213 — Resolution\n- Status: Resolved\n- Resolution notes: \"Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent — admin-mediated reset per 